<methodology>
Backward taint analysis from object-binding sinks.
1. Sinks. Model(**request.data) / .objects.create(**body), update(**params), setattr loops over
   request keys, Object.assign(obj, req.body), spread {...req.body}, serializer without fields/
   read_only, ORM mass-update from a full payload.
2. Sources. Full request body/params bound without field selection.
3. Adequate control. An explicit allowlist of writable fields (serializer fields, DTO, pick()/
   permit()) that excludes sensitive attributes.
4. Impact. Trace whether any privileged/sensitive field is reachable: role/permissions,
   ownership, monetary, or state fields.
</methodology>

<examples>
Illustrative vulnerable patterns (different languages; NOT specific to this repo —
do not assume these files exist here; use them to recognise the shape of the bug):
  - python: `User.objects.create(**request.data)`
    why: every posted key becomes a field; a client can set is_staff/is_superuser.
  - ruby: `user.update(params[:user])`
    why: without strong params, role/admin can be over-posted.
  - javascript: `Object.assign(user, req.body)`
    why: the whole body is merged onto the user, including unintended fields.
</examples>

<false_positives_to_avoid>
- An explicit field allowlist (DRF `fields=`, Rails `permit`, a hand-picked dict) is adequate.
- Binding to a DTO that contains only safe fields is not a finding.
- Read-only/server-set fields excluded by the serializer are safe.
</false_positives_to_avoid>

Confidence: High = clear unprotected path, deterministic. Medium = strongly
indicated but a material uncertainty remains. Low = plausible but the trace is
incomplete. Rule: when uncertain, round down (favour Medium/Low).

Begin by reading the entry-point files above (or, if none, list_dir the scope),
then trace each candidate toward the sinks above.

